  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cool and mostly dry weather through early Tuesday \nmorning.\n\nA strong northern stream trough will move across the eastern Great \nLakes region today, bringing northwest flow and cold air advection \nthat will result in a cooler day. 850mb temperatures will drop to \naround +4 deg C by this afternoon. Due to a dry airmass, \nminimal lake effect response is expected, though lake effect \nclouds and widely scattered showers are possible along the I-90 \ncorridor this morning. Surface high pressure will build from the\nwest, bringing widespread dry weather this afternoon through \ntonight. \n\nTemperatures today will be about 10 degrees cooler than Sunday, with \nhighs in the mid to upper 60s, low 60s across the higher terrain. \nEfficient radiational cooling will allow temperatures to drop into \nthe 40s, with upper 30s in the interior valleys of the Southern Tier \nand Black River Valley of Lewis county. Patchy frost is possible in \nfar southern Lewis county late tonight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Periodic showers and thunderstorms are possible \nalong a stationary front Tuesday night through Thursday night. \n\nSurface high pressure will move off the New England coast Tuesday. A \nnorthern stream trough will move across central Canada/Hudson Bay \nthrough mid-week. A cold front associated with low pressure near \nHudson Bay will approach the region Tuesday. Warm air advection and \na surface warm front will result in high temperatures moving back \ninto the 70s. A few showers can't be ruled out across the Niagara \nFrontier Tuesday afternoon, with dry weather expected elsewhere. \n\nThe trough will move by to the north through Thursday and zonal flow \nis expected across the region. The cold front is expected to sag \nsouthward across the forecast area late Tuesday night through \nWednesday, becoming stationary through Thursday. A corridor of deep \nmoisture along the front is expected to settle across the region \nwith the potential for PWATS over 1.6\". Chances for showers will \nincrease beginning Tuesday night. The stationary front will become \nthe focus of precipitation Wednesday through Thursday, however there \nis some uncertainity in the forecast due to the potential for\nthe front to oscillate. Guidance suggests a wave of low \npressure may move along the front and bring the potential for \nwidespread showers and a few thunderstorms to the forecast area \nlate Wednesday night through Thursday. \n\nThe front is expected to move south of the region Thursday night \ninto Friday. Surface high pressure will support drier weather Friday \ninto the weekend.",
